\chapter{Custom Literals: Registering Suffixes}
\label{ch:custom-literals}

\section{Two Ways to Write a Literal}

A units language wants to write \code{3.5_km} and mean three and a half
kilometres. There are two entirely different mechanisms that can make that
work, and the distinction between them is the distinction between a
\emph{registered} suffix and a \emph{native} user-defined literal.

A native user-defined literal is part of the \cpp{} language. The programmer
writes \code{operator""_km(long double)} in a namespace, and the compiler
resolves the token \code{3.5_km} by ordinary overload resolution at the
point where the token is written. The resolution happens once, during
translation, and the result is a constant expression. The suffix is a
property of the \emph{token}, not of the text.

A registered suffix is a run-time facility. The programmer registers a
string such as \code{"_km"} together with a handler, and later hands the
library a \code{std::string_view} containing \code{"3.5_km"}. The library
finds the suffix by comparing the tail of the string against the registered
suffixes, converts the remaining prefix to a number, and calls the handler.
The resolution happens once per call, at run time, and the suffix is a
property of the \emph{text}.

\DSLtk{} provides both routes, and its own quick-start comment recommends
the native form when true literal syntax is what is wanted. The registered
route exists because a DSL frequently has to read literals from a
configuration file, a command line, or a token stream, where no compile-time
token is available at all.

\section{A Worked Example}

Listing~\ref{lst:ch18-units} is a complete program that uses both routes in
the same translation unit. Three suffixes are \emph{registered}, so that
strings arriving at run time can be interpreted; the same three suffixes are
also declared as \emph{native} literals, so that a literal written directly
in the source is resolved by the compiler.

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcpp,caption={A units DSL with three registered suffixes and three native user-defined literals. The registered set is consulted by \code{parse_literal}, which the \code{CustomLiterals} mixin adds as a static member; the native literals are resolved by the compiler at the point of use.},label={lst:ch18-units}]
#include "DSLtk.hpp"
#include <iostream>
#include <stdexcept>

// ---------------------------------------------------------------
// A DSL with three *registered* suffixes.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------
struct UnitsDSL : dsl::DSL<UnitsDSL, dsl::CustomLiterals>
{
  static constexpr auto literals = dsl::literal_set (
      dsl::lit<"_km"> ([] (long double v) { return v * 1000.0L; }),
      dsl::lit<"_m"> ([] (long double v) { return v; }),
      dsl::lit<"_cm"> ([] (long double v) { return v / 100.0L; }));
};

// ---------------------------------------------------------------
// A *native* user-defined literal, in an inline namespace.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------
inline namespace unit_literals
{
  constexpr long double operator""_km (long double v) { return v * 1000.0L; }
  constexpr long double operator""_m (long double v) { return v; }
  constexpr long double operator""_cm (long double v) { return v / 100.0L; }
}

int main ()
{
  std::cout.precision (10);

  // Registered suffixes: a runtime scan over a string.
  std::cout << "parse_literal(\"3.5_km\")  = "
            << UnitsDSL::parse_literal ("3.5_km") << "\n";
  std::cout << "parse_literal(\"2_m\")    = "
            << UnitsDSL::parse_literal ("2_m") << "\n";
  std::cout << "parse_literal(\"50_cm\")  = "
            << UnitsDSL::parse_literal ("50_cm") << "\n";

  // Native user-defined literals: resolved by the compiler.
  std::cout << "3.5_km (native)          = " << 3.5_km << "\n";
  std::cout << "2.0_m (native)          = " << 2.0_m << "\n";

  // Failure mode 1: nothing matched.
  try
    {
      UnitsDSL::parse_literal ("7_furlong");
    }
  catch (const std::runtime_error &e)
    {
      std::cout << "runtime_error: " << e.what () << "\n";
    }

  // Failure mode 2: the numeric prefix is not a number.
  try
    {
      UnitsDSL::parse_literal ("abc_km");
    }
  catch (const std::invalid_argument &)
    {
      std::cout << "invalid_argument thrown by std::stold\n";
    }
}
\end{lstlisting}

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcmd,caption={Output of Listing~\ref{lst:ch18-units}. The registered and the native route agree on all three suffixes, because the handlers were written to agree; nothing enforces that they must.},label={lst:ch18-units-out}]
parse_literal("3.5_km")  = 3500
parse_literal("2_m")    = 2
parse_literal("50_cm")  = 0.5
3.5_km (native)          = 3500
2.0_m (native)          = 2
runtime_error: dsl::LiteralSet::parse: no suffix matched for '7_furlong'
invalid_argument thrown by std::stold
\end{lstlisting}

The two mechanisms are entirely independent. Nothing checks that the
registered set and the native literal set agree, and nothing stops a program
from registering a suffix that has no native counterpart, or from writing a
native literal whose suffix is not registered. A disciplined DSL keeps the
two in step by hand, or by generating one from the other; \DSLtk{} offers no
mechanism for doing so.

\section{The Data Model}

The facility consists of two types, two factories, and one feature tag.

\code{dsl::LiteralEntry<Suffix, F>} (lines 7141--7147) is one registered
suffix. Its first template parameter is a \code{FixedString}, so the suffix
text is fixed at compile time; its second is the handler type. The entry
holds the handler by value in a member named \code{handler}, and exposes the
suffix through a \emph{static} member
\code{static constexpr std::string_view suffix}. The static is not an
oversight: every entry of a given suffix type shares the same string, and
the set does not need to store it per instance.

\code{dsl::LiteralSet<Entries...>} (lines 7177--7229) is the set. It holds a
\code{std::tuple<Entries...>}, so the number of registered suffixes and
their types are fixed at compile time. The only member function is
\code{parse}, which is \code{const} and returns \code{long double}.

\code{dsl::lit<Suffix>(f)} (lines 7158--7163) is the factory for an entry.
It takes the handler by forwarding reference and decays it, so that the
entry's type is
\code{LiteralEntry<Suffix, std::decay_t<F>>}.
The companion factory \code{dsl::literal_set(...)} (lines 7240--7245) builds
the set, decaying each entry in turn, and so returns
\code{LiteralSet<std::decay_t<Entries>...>}.

\code{dsl::CustomLiterals} (lines 7265--7281) is the feature tag. Its
mixin contributes a single \emph{static} member function,
\code{parse_literal(std::string_view)}, guarded by the constraint
\code{requires requires \{ Derived::literals; \}}. The guard means the
member exists only when the deriving type actually has a \code{literals}
member; a DSL that names the feature but supplies no set simply does not
get the function, and the error appears at the point of the missing member
rather than as a link failure.

