Table~\ref{tab:ch18-api} is the whole surface. There is no visitor, no
registry keyed by string at run time, no way to add a suffix after the set
has been constructed, and no way to enumerate the registered suffixes other
than by inspecting the tuple.

\section{The Resolution Algorithm}

\code{LiteralSet::parse} is short, and its behaviour is entirely determined
by four lines. The header expresses it with \code{std::apply} over the
tuple, and a comma fold over the pack; the effect is a fold over the
entries in declaration order.

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcpp,caption={The body of \code{LiteralSet::parse}, transcribed from \code{DSLtk.hpp} lines 7192--7228. The comma fold in \code{std::apply} evaluates every entry's lambda in turn; each lambda returns immediately if a match has already been found.},label={lst:ch18-parse}]
  long double
  parse (std::string_view input) const
  {
    long double result = 0.0L;
    bool found = false;

    std::apply (
        [&] (const auto &...e)
          {
            (
                [&]
                  {
                    if (found)
                      return;
                    auto suf = e.suffix;
                    if (input.size () > suf.size ()
                        && input.substr (input.size () - suf.size ()) == suf)
                      {
                        auto num_str
                            = input.substr (0, input.size () - suf.size ());
                        long double num = std::stold (std::string (num_str));
                        result = e.handler (num);
                        found = true;
                      }
                  }(),
                ...);
          },
        entries);

    if (!found)
      {
        throw std::runtime_error (
            std::string ("dsl::LiteralSet::parse: no suffix matched for '")
            + std::string (input) + "'");
      }
    return result;
  }
\end{lstlisting}

Algorithm~\ref{alg:ch18-resolve} restates the procedure without the
template machinery. The points that matter are these.

\begin{pseudocode}[Resolving a suffix in a literal set]
\textbf{function} resolve (set, input)\\
\quad found := false\\
\quad \textbf{for each} entry \textbf{in} set.entries, in declaration order do\\
\qquad \textbf{if} found \textbf{then continue}\\
\qquad suffix := entry.suffix\\
\qquad \textbf{if} length (input) > length (suffix) \textbf{and}\\
\qquad\qquad tail (input, length (suffix)) = suffix \textbf{then}\\
\qquad\qquad prefix := input without its last length (suffix) characters\\
\qquad\qquad number := std::stold (prefix)\\
\qquad\qquad result := entry.handler (number)\\
\qquad\qquad found := true\\
\quad \textbf{if not} found \textbf{then}\\
\qquad \textbf{raise} runtime\_error ("dsl::LiteralSet::parse: no suffix matched")\\
\quad \textbf{return} result
\label{alg:ch18-resolve}
\end{pseudocode}

First, the suffix test is a plain tail comparison. There is no regular
expression, no tokenisation, and no notion of a numeric token; the test is
\code{input.size () > suf.size () && input.substr (input.size () - suf.size ()) == suf}.
The strict inequality is a design decision with a visible consequence: the
input must be strictly longer than the suffix, so a bare suffix with no
numeric prefix never matches. The input \code{"_km"} therefore raises the
"no suffix matched" error rather than being read as the number zero.

Second, the scan is over the tuple in declaration order, and the
\emph{first} entry whose suffix matches wins. This is not obvious from the
source, because the comma fold in \code{std::apply} evaluates \emph{every}
entry's lambda rather than stopping at the first match. The early exit is
achieved by the guard at the top of each lambda: \code{if (found) return;}.
The net effect is first-match-wins, and the guard makes the fold's
evaluation order observable only in the cost, not in the result.

Third, the numeric prefix is converted with \code{std::stold}, and the
handler is applied to the resulting number, not to the string. The handler's
argument is always a \code{long double}.

Fourth, and slightly surprisingly, \code{parse} always returns a
\code{long double}, whatever the handler returns. The result variable is
declared as \code{long double result = 0.0L;} and the handler's return value
is assigned into it. A handler that returns \code{int} or \code{double} is
converted; a handler that returns something not convertible to
\code{long double} is a compile error.

Figure~\ref{fig:ch18-scan} draws the scan for the input \code{"3.5_km"}
against a set whose first entry is \code{"_km"}.

