\section{Failure Modes}
\label{sec:ch18-failures}

Every failure of the facility is an exception, and the exceptions are
precisely specified by what the underlying standard-library functions throw.
Table~\ref{tab:ch18-failures} enumerates them.

\begin{table}[ht]
\centering
\caption{Every outcome of \code{LiteralSet::parse}, with the registered set
holding the kilometre, metre and centimetre suffixes. The exception types are
those of the underlying standard-library calls and are not wrapped.}
\label{tab:ch18-failures}
\small
\begin{tabular}{@{}L{0.26\textwidth}L{0.34\textwidth}L{0.32\textwidth}@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Input} & \textbf{Outcome} & \textbf{Exception} \\
\midrule
\code{"3.5_km"} & \code{3500} & none \\
\code{"2_m"} & \code{2} & none \\
\code{"3.5"} & no suffix matched & \code{std::runtime_error} \\
\code{"_km"} & no suffix matched & \code{std::runtime_error} \\
\code{"7_furlong"} & no suffix matched & \code{std::runtime_error} \\
\code{"abc_km"} & numeric prefix is not a number & \code{std::invalid_argument} \\
\code{"1e9999_km"} & number out of range for \code{long double} & \code{std::out_of_range} \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

The message of the "no suffix matched" error is fixed by the header and
names the offending input:
\code{"dsl::LiteralSet::parse: no suffix matched for '7_furlong'"}. The
other two exceptions are \emph{not} wrapped. They arrive from
\code{std::stold} unchanged, which means a caller cannot distinguish them
by type from a \code{dsl::}-specific failure, but can by message: the
standard library's \code{std::invalid_argument} from \code{stold} carries
only the name of the function.

One more subtlety is worth spelling out, because it is easy to trip over.
\code{std::stold} does not require the whole string to be a number. It
performs the longest valid conversion and ignores whatever follows. So
\code{std::stold("3.5_")} is \code{3.5}, not an error, and
\code{std::stold("3.5x")} is also \code{3.5}. The only case in which
\code{std::invalid_argument} is thrown is when \emph{no} conversion is
possible at all, as with \code{"abc"}. This has a practical consequence for
suffix sets whose suffixes overlap: if \code{"km"} is registered before
\code{"_km"}, then the input \code{"3.5_km"} matches \code{"km"} first,
the numeric prefix becomes \code{"3.5_"}, and \code{std::stold} silently
returns \code{3.5} instead of failing. The result is a plausible number
derived from the wrong suffix.

\section{Registered Suffix or Native Literal?}

The header's own quick-start comment shows both in Example~A (lines
273--288): a \code{UnitsDSL} with a registered set, and an inline namespace
containing \code{operator""_km} and \code{operator""_m}. Table~\ref{tab:ch18-compare}
sets the two mechanisms side by side.

\begin{table}[ht]
\centering
\caption{Registered suffixes and native user-defined literals, compared.}
\label{tab:ch18-compare}
\small
\begin{tabular}{@{}L{0.22\textwidth}L{0.35\textwidth}L{0.35\textwidth}@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Aspect} & \textbf{Registered suffix} & \textbf{Native literal} \\
\midrule
When resolved & at run time, on each call & at compile time, during translation \\
Input & a \code{std::string_view} & a token in the source \\
Matching & tail comparison against a compile-time-known set & overload resolution on the literal's type and value \\
Failure & an exception at run time & a compile error \\
Cost & a scan over the registered entries & nothing \\
Reach & any string, from a file or a command line & only text written in the source \\
Suffix set & extensible per DSL, at compile time & fixed by the overloads visible at the point of use \\
Return type & always \code{long double} & whatever the literal operator returns \\
\midrule
\multicolumn{3}{@{}L{0.92\textwidth}@{}}{\emph{Reserved suffixes}: the
standard reserves a family of suffixes for future standardisation, including
the string suffix \code{s} and the SI prefixes. Suffixes such as
\code{"_km"}, \code{"_m"} and \code{"_cm"} are free to use.} \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

The practical difference is best understood in terms of where the text comes
from. A native literal is available only where the programmer writes the
token; a configuration file that contains the line \code{distance = 3.5_km}
offers no token for the compiler to resolve. That is the situation the
registered facility exists for, and it is why \DSLtk{} provides it at all.

There are two further differences that surprise people.

The first is that a native literal for a \code{long double} parameter applies
only to \emph{floating-point} literals. The token \code{2.0_m} resolves
against \code{operator""_m(long double)}, but the token \code{2_m} does
not: for an integer literal the compiler looks for an overload taking
\code{unsigned long long}, and if it finds none the literal is an error.
Writing \code{2_m} therefore requires a second overload with an integral
parameter, which is not something the header provides.

The second is that a native literal is a constant expression. It can be used
in a \code{constexpr} context, in a template argument, in an array bound,
and in a \code{static_assert}. A registered suffix produces a value at run
time and can be used in none of those places.

The D port offers the same two mechanisms with the same split. Its
\code{literal_set} and \code{lit} are at lines 486--499 of \code{DSLtk.d},
its \code{CustomLiterals} mixin adds \code{static real parse_literal} at
line 112, and its exception message reads
\code{"DSLtk.LiteralSet.parse: no suffix matched for '...'"} rather than the
\cpp{} spelling. The D implementation returns at the first match directly,
using a \code{static foreach} loop, so it does not share the \cpp{} quirk of
evaluating every entry's lambda; the observable semantics are the same.

\section{Why the Suffix Is a Compile-Time Constant}

The suffix text is a template argument of type \code{FixedString}, and that
single design decision has consequences in both directions.

On the favourable side, the set of candidate suffixes is fixed when the
program is compiled. The scan of Algorithm~\ref{alg:ch18-resolve} walks a
tuple whose length and whose element types are both known to the compiler,
so there is no dynamic dispatch through a registry, no string comparison
against a list built at run time, and no possibility of a handler being
registered after the set was constructed. The cost of the scan is therefore
a handful of string comparisons against compile-time constants, and the
compiler can unroll it.

The suffix text is also part of the \emph{type}. \code{LiteralEntry<"_km",
F>} and \code{LiteralEntry<"_m", F>} are different types, and the same
handler type may appear under several suffixes without any of them being
confused for another. A program that wants to know, at compile time, which
suffix an entry carries can ask the type.

On the unfavourable side, the suffix cannot come from a configuration file.
A tool that lets its user define \code{"_furlong"} at run time cannot use
this facility for that suffix, because there is no way to build a
\code{LiteralEntry} whose \code{FixedString} is not known when the program
is translated. The facility is for suffixes that are part of the language,
which is exactly what a DSL wants; it is not for suffixes that are part of
a deployment.

\section{Composing the Literal Set with Other Features}

The \code{CustomLiterals} mixin contributes a single static member and
nothing else, so it composes with the other features in the ordinary way:
the mixins are independent bases, and a DSL may name as many as it likes.
The shipped example \code{18-mini-dsl.cpp} combines the literal facility
with the pipeline feature, and it is worth reading because it shows the
mixins doing independent jobs.

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcpp,caption={The shipped example \code{18-mini-dsl.cpp}, reproduced verbatim. The literal set and the pipeline are independent features of the same DSL type; the literal set is consulted by the static \code{parse_literal}, and the pipeline then transforms the value it produced.},label={lst:ch18-mini}]
#include "DSLtk.hpp"
#include <iostream>

struct MiniDSL : dsl::DSL<MiniDSL, dsl::Pipeline, dsl::CustomLiterals> {
  static constexpr auto literals = dsl::literal_set(
      dsl::lit<"_km">([](long double v) { return v * 1000.0L; }),
      dsl::lit<"_m">([](long double v) { return v; }));
};

int main() {
  auto meters = MiniDSL::parse_literal("2.5_km");
  auto shown = MiniDSL::wrap(meters) | dsl::pipe([](long double x) { return x + 10.0L; });
  std::cout << shown << "\n";
}
\end{lstlisting}

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcmd,caption={Output of Listing~\ref{lst:ch18-mini}. The registered suffix converts 2.5 kilometres to 2500 metres, and the pipeline stage then adds ten.},label={lst:ch18-mini-out}]
2510
\end{lstlisting}

Two details of this example are worth pointing out. The value that
\code{parse_literal} produces is a \code{long double}, not an \code{int}
and not a user-defined unit type; the pipeline stage therefore has to
accept a \code{long double}, and the unit information is lost as soon as
the suffix has been resolved. A DSL that wants to keep the unit would need
a handler that returns a unit-tagged type, but \code{parse} would then have
to convert that type to a \code{long double}, and it does not. The facility
as shipped normalises every literal to a plain number.

Second, the suffix strings in the example are written without a leading
space and are matched by a plain tail comparison, which means that the
suffix \code{"_m"} would also match a string ending in the letters
\code{"_m"} anywhere, and the suffix \code{"m"} would match almost
anything. The convention of beginning every suffix with an underscore is
not enforced by the library, but it is what keeps the tail comparison
unambiguous in practice.

\section{Edge Cases and Limits}

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{The suffix must be strictly shorter than the input.} The test
is \code{input.size () > suf.size ()}, not \code{>=}. A suffix with no
numeric prefix never matches, and the resulting error is the generic "no
suffix matched" one rather than a diagnostic about a missing number.

\item \textbf{An empty suffix matches everything.} \code{dsl::lit<"">} is
accepted by the compiler; the C++ port performs no check on the suffix
length. Since the tail of any non-empty input of length zero is the empty
string, such an entry matches the first entry position and its handler is
applied to the whole input. The D port does check: \code{dsl::lit} in
\code{DSLtk.d} carries a \code{static assert(suffix.length > 0)}.

\item \textbf{Overlapping suffixes are resolved by declaration order, not by
length.} Nothing sorts the entries, and nothing prefers the longest match.
Registering \code{"km"} before \code{"_km"} changes the meaning of
\code{"3.5_km"}, as Section~\ref{sec:ch18-failures} showed. The safe
convention is to register longer suffixes first, or to give every suffix a
distinct leading delimiter.

\item \textbf{Trailing rubbish in the numeric prefix is silently ignored.}
\code{std::stold} accepts a valid numeric prefix followed by anything. The
header does not validate that the whole prefix was consumed.

\item \textbf{The return type is fixed.} \code{parse} returns
\code{long double}. A handler that computes an integer, a rational, or a
quantity with a unit will be truncated to a \code{long double}, silently.

\item \textbf{There is no thread-safety story and no shared state.} A
\code{LiteralSet} is an immutable value once constructed; two threads may
call \code{parse} on the same set concurrently without harm, provided the
handlers themselves are re-entrant.

\item \textbf{Handlers are stored by value.} A \code{LiteralSet} owns copies
of its handlers. A handler that captures a large object by value is copied
into the set, and the set itself is copied whenever it is copied.

\item \textbf{The feature mixin adds only a static member.} There is no
non-static overload, no instance-level \code{parse_literal}, and no way to
install a set at run time. The set is a \code{static constexpr} member of
the deriving type, which means the handlers must be constant-initialisable.

\item \textbf{A native literal cannot be applied to a string.} The
registered facility accepts arbitrary text, including text with whitespace
or a unit spelled out in full; a native literal can only ever be a numeric
token followed by its suffix.
\end{itemize}

\section{Closing}

The custom-literal facility is small and easy to use: register a suffix and
a handler, build a set, and hand strings to it. Its limitations are equally
clear. Resolution is a linear scan at run time, matching is a tail
comparison with no notion of numeric tokens, the result is always a
\code{long double}, and every failure is an exception inherited from the
standard library. For a DSL that must read units out of a configuration
file, it is exactly the right tool; for a DSL whose literals appear in
source code, the native user-defined literal is both simpler and strictly
